{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":145095,"duration_api_ms":242748,"num_turns":5,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"e75690d4-6cc7-425a-bdd8-d51226953ebb","total_cost_usd":0.7096246500000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29606,"cache_read_input_tokens":82852,"output_tokens":3493,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29606,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":236,"outputTokens":13581,"cacheReadInputTokens":1763339,"cacheCreationInputTokens":121073,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.39581615,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":3493,"cacheReadInputTokens":82852,"cacheCreationInputTokens":29606,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.31380850000000005,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"All six amendments are verified against the codebase. The chemistry-correctness fixes (fr-2-46f297bc, fr-2-21162875) address real thermodynamic inconsistencies reachable in normal simulation. The contract-compliance fix (fr-2-26ba2577) closes a documented-but-unimplemented requirement. The validation unification (fr-2-102f3c3c) fixes a genuine contract split where identical chemistry is accepted or rejected depending on entry path. The test gap (fr-2-234f259a) and dead-code cleanup (fr-2-88ff4519) are lower severity but technically sound. I accept all six.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-2-46f297bc","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: fallback_all_co2 (chemistry.rs:85-92) assigns all DIC to CO2 with zero bicarbonate/carbonate, but computes pH via acid_only_fallback_ph which assumes partial dissociation. At the clamped pH of 5.5, Henderson-Hasselbalch gives ~12% HCO3- relative to CO2, so the species vector is thermodynamically inconsistent with the reported pH. carbonate_species_for_ph exists at lines 65-72 and could reproject species at the solved pH. This inconsistency artificially zeros bicarbonate_mg_total in low-alkalinity regimes, distorting downstream TDS/conductivity. The fix is straightforward and correct."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-26ba2577","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: docs/carbonate_state_contract.md line 112 explicitly states fallback paths should 'emit a structured diagnostic'. Lines 146 reinforces this. All three fallback functions (fallback_all_co2, fallback_high_buffer, fallback_outside_quadratic_envelope) return silently with no logging. tank_core's Cargo.toml has no tracing dependency, confirming zero instrumentation. Since fallback paths materially alter chemistry behavior (clamping pH, zeroing species), silent operation violates the design contract and makes edge-case debugging impossible. Adding tracing::debug! requires adding the tracing dependency but is a minimal, justified change."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-234f259a","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: Existing tests in materialization.rs verify that profiles exist in the catalog and check calcium/temperature, but no test asserts that the four shipped profiles produce differentiated pH values through the runtime solver path (WaterState::from_source_profile_for_volume_l → resolve_carbonate_state → solve_carbonate_equilibrium). Since preset validation uses a separate predicted_carbonate_ph implementation while runtime uses the full solver, an integration test asserting pH ordering and minimum spread would catch regressions that neither path's unit tests would detect independently."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-88ff4519","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: migrate_v2_to_v3 (save.rs:298) includes bicarbonate_mg_total in dissolved_fields for rescaling, but normalize_loaded_carbonate_state (save.rs:345) unconditionally calls resolve_carbonate_state which overwrites bicarbonate_mg_total from DIC+alkalinity. Further confirmed by rescale_totals_for_volume (water.rs:184-208) which intentionally excludes bicarbonate from rescaling then calls resolve_carbonate_state. The migration rescale is provably dead work. Removing it with a comment that bicarbonate is solver-derived prevents future migration authors from copying the pattern and thinking it's load-bearing."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-21162875","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: NEUTRAL_FALLBACK (chemistry.rs:50-55) returns pH 7.0 when DIC ≤ 1e-12. With high alkalinity and arbitrarily small positive DIC, the solver can return pH up to 8.5 (the storage clamp ceiling). Four separate paths clamp DIC to exactly zero: chemistry.rs:310, plant_growth.rs:149, algae_growth.rs:115, algae_growth.rs:196. A strongly photosynthetic tank consuming all DIC will trigger one of these clamps, causing a one-tick pH snap from ~8.5 to 7.0. This is a reachable discontinuity in normal simulation, not a theoretical concern. The zero-DIC branch should return a limit-consistent pH (approaching the value the solver gives for DIC→0+ under the current alkalinity) rather than snapping to neutral."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-102f3c3c","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: presets.rs:150-169 validates source water by calling predicted_carbonate_ph() which rejects zero-alkalinity/nonzero-DIC via the alk ≤ 1e-9 branch at line 44, and enforces pH range 5.5-8.5. Runtime validation in source_water.rs:31-66 only checks finiteness and non-negativity — no pH constraint, no carbonate consistency check. The same SourceWaterProfile payload can therefore be rejected when loaded from TOML but accepted when it arrives via save deserialization or runtime catalog. This is a genuine contract split that could cause confusing behavior (presets fail validation but equivalent runtime profiles simulate fine). Unifying both paths behind one shared validation rule is the correct fix."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"7bb2502f-e67b-4d2e-babf-f990f8c16f99"}
